{% set source = "the paper" if paper_level else "the papers" %}`independent_family_count` (≤ family_count): of the qualifying families, how many are independent observations of the gene–disease link. Criteria A/B/C use this count. Go through the qualifying families variant by variant: first the MORE-THAN-10 RULE, then, for the variants it does not cover, the rules for smaller groups. Every family is counted once.

MORE-THAN-10 RULE (apply first): a variant in more than 10 families counts every family. Count the qualifying families that carry the variant{% if not paper_level %}, over all contributing papers{% endif %}: de novo and inherited families together, homozygous and compound heterozygous families together, whatever their country or ancestry. When they are MORE THAN 10, each of them counts as 1. This holds also when a shared haplotype shows one ancestral origin, and it is decided on all the families that carry the variant, before any of them is set aside under the rules for smaller groups. A variant in that many families is too large a signal to fold into a single data point. Record the shared origin as a founder caveat. (A single consanguineous pedigree remains ONE family and never reaches this rule.)

A VARIANT IN 10 OR FEWER FAMILIES:
- a family whose qualifying variant no other family carries → 1;
- each de novo–confirmed family (parents wild-type) → 1, even if the variant recurs (a de novo event is a new origin; recurrent de novo is the strongest evidence);
- the inherited (non–de novo) families that share the variant, by the FIRST matching rule:
  1. shared haplotype shown across the carriers → 1 (one proven origin);
  2. otherwise group the families by the country or ancestry {{ source }} report{% if paper_level %}s{% endif %} for them: each distinct country or ancestry counts 1 (a single founder allele rarely spans distinct ancestries), and all the families without a reported country or ancestry count 1 together. Families of one community, one consanguineous cluster or one broad population are one group: missing or shared ancestry does NOT make families independent.

Founder/recurrence is a confidence caveat (`founder_or_recurrent_variant`); the shared-origin collapse above is its only count effect — do not penalise twice, and it never feeds Criterion E.

Always set `count_reduction_reasoning` to the qualifying and independent counts plus the per-group disposition, e.g. "7 qualifying: 5 distinct variants → 5; L14V in 2 Han/Shanghai families, no haplotype → 1; independent 6", or "677 qualifying: c.2204+6T>C in 677 families → more-than-10 rule, 677; independent 677". For every variant that two or more families share, give each family's country or ancestry as {{ source }} report{% if paper_level %}s{% endif %} it, or say that it is not reported{% if not paper_level %}; take it from the papers' `count_reduction_reasoning` and summaries{% endif %}. Write "No reduction (N families)" only when the counts are equal and no family was derived under COHORTS WITHOUT FAMILY STRUCTURE.
